  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 304 AM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nFrom Friday night onward, an active period is in store as near-zonal \nto northwesterly flow returns. This will bring chances for showers \nand storms near-daily for parts of the area.\n\nFriday Night and Saturday... Following a brief bout of ridging, an \nupper level shortwave will bring the next chances for showers and \nstorms late Friday night into Saturday. While the bulk of forcing \nwill remain to the north, a frontal boundary will provide enough \nforcing for showers and isolated storms as it plows into a pool of \nmodest instability. \n\nAlso on Saturday, a strong shortwave will move into the Pacific \nNorthwest, which will shape the forecast Sunday through Wednesday. \nWhile models present a number of solutions with regard to timing and \ntrack, all agree that this system will be our next chance for \nsubstantial rainfall. As this trough strengthens over the \nIntermountain West, ridging over the north-central CONUS will bring \nus the potential for yet another day of above-normal temperatures on \nMonday. This will also help support growing instability ahead of the \nsystem. This, along with vertical shear profiles, will support the \npotential for severe thunderstorms. In fact, SPC has issued a \nSlight, level 2 of 5, risk of severe thunderstorms for the far \nwestern edge of the CWA for Monday.\n\nBehind this system, a cooler but still active period with near-daily \nchances for rainfall is expected.",
